  "summary": "One machine with 200GB of room and every Booking position on it taken, and the impossible ask is the oldest wait in the deployment. Nothing here can hold a Run asking for 900GB, so the fleet's own answer exempts it from the queue: the experimental Run that arrives a minute later fits this machine, waits for a position on it, and is not ordered behind an ask no machine can satisfy. It outranks the standard ask, so the ask is then deferred behind it, and what Mercator records about that moment is a wait the queue caused. The record says what it rests on, which is nothing: no machine was weighed, so nothing was established about capacity at all. An exemption is a claim about the fleet, and a wait that asked the fleet nothing leaves none behind. Carrying the last one forward was a claim about a fleet nobody had asked since, and a Run outranked by a steady stream of arrivals never asks again: work of its own class that arrived later overtook it, for as long as the stream lasted, however much capacity had been added meanwhile. So the last Run here is told it waits behind both, which costs it nothing it had not already lost. It only fails to renew an exemption while something outranks it, and whatever it would have been holding up is held up by that work anyway. The whole set is the assertion: the exemption that stops one impossible submission from emptying a fleet is only ever as old as the answer it was read off.",
